#!/usr/bin/env bash
# mn — run THIS working tree's mnemosyne-cli, always built from the current
# source, never resolved from `~/.cargo/bin`.
#
# THE ONE RESOLVER for "the CLI of this checkout". Before Round 823 there were
# three: `.githooks/pre-commit` and `.githooks/pre-push` each carried a
# byte-identical chain (release binary if present -> PATH -> `cargo run`), and
# interactive use just typed `mnemosyne-cli`, which is the PATH entry. Both of
# those resolutions could hand back the wrong tool:
#
#   - `target/release/mnemosyne-cli` was preferred WITHOUT a freshness check, so
#     a binary from an older build could silently gate a commit;
#   - `~/.cargo/bin/mnemosyne-cli` is a SHARED slot. A sibling checkout installs
#     its own pinned rev there and we overwrite it with ours (that collision is
#     real and was ours: on 2026-07-29 this repo's `cargo install --path --force`
#     replaced a consumer's pinned `75eddce5` with an uncommitted local build).
#     A tool nobody agreed on builds a store nobody can reproduce.
#
# So: no PATH, no install, no preference for a stale artifact. Ask cargo, which
# already owns the question "is this build current" — never re-implement
# staleness here, that would be a second answer free to drift from cargo's.
#
# DEBUG, not release, and that is measured rather than assumed (Round 823). A
# hook runs immediately after `git add`, so the git index has just moved and the
# version stamp must be recomputed; from there a release build costs ~50s while
# debug costs ~4s, and debug shares its artifacts with the clippy run the
# pre-commit hook already does. Release wins only on execution (0.19s vs 1.0s on
# this store), which does not repay a 46s build. If a caller ever needs the fast
# binary for a long run, build it explicitly — this script is about being RIGHT.
#
# Usage:  scripts/mn <any mnemosyne-cli args>
#         scripts/mn validate-workspace
#
# The build happens in this repo; the command runs in the CALLER's directory, so
# `scripts/mn validate-workspace` from a consumer's workspace validates THEIRS.
# AND THIS BUILD IS HELD TO WHAT THE HOSTED JOB HOLDS THE TREE TO (Round 1342).
#
# THE EVIDENCE IS THAT IT WAS PRINTED TWICE AND READ PAST TWICE. Round 1337 left
# two unused imports in `mnemosyne-cli`; the hosted `validate` job died on them
# at `cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings` and the eight steps behind it never
# ran. This script compiled that crate and printed both warnings — to the round
# that shipped them, and again to the round that repaired them. What it lacked
# was not a check but a REFUSAL.
#
# WHY HERE, of all the places a strictness could go: since Round 1287 the git
# hooks compile nothing, deliberately (Round 1284 measured a commit that could
# not finish eleven times while sibling repositories held the cores), so the
# hosted clippy is the only thing that judges warnings — one round later. This
# build is the ONE local compile that always happens, because every hook runs
# the CLI through this script. Making it strict adds no compilation that was not
# already happening.
#
# WHAT IT COSTS, MEASURED ON THIS MACHINE RATHER THAN ASSUMED (2026-09-15).
# `RUSTFLAGS` is part of a unit's fingerprint, so the strict build keeps its own
# artifacts: the first one costs 19.1s, every repeat costs 0.10s, and the
# artifacts of a plain `cargo build` SURVIVE it (0.08s immediately after), so
# nothing else in this repository rebuilds because of this line. The price is one
# rebuild of this crate per source change, on the path that was already building
# it, against a red that otherwise reaches `main` and is read a round later.
#
# AND THERE IS NO ESCAPE HATCH, which is the part to argue with rather than work
# around: a round that is mid-refactor and has a warning cannot talk to its own
# store until it resolves it. That is the intended shape — a warning here is the
# same warning CI will refuse — and an environment variable that waved it through
# would be read by the next round as the way to keep working.
set -euo pipefail

repo="$(cd "$(dirname "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)"
(cd "$repo" && RUSTFLAGS="${RUSTFLAGS:+$RUSTFLAGS }-D warnings" cargo build -q --locked -p mnemosyne-cli)
exec "$repo/target/debug/mnemosyne-cli" "$@"
